{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CggUTXvUCXSu2aeTd3qSx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fitting the three bands gives **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. Treat these as approximate, since they come from an octahedral-model mapping rather than a direct tetrahedral calculation.\n\n**Approach**\n- Tetrahedral d⁷ high-spin Co(II) has a ⁴A₂ ground state. The three spin-allowed bands are assigned in order as ν₁ = ⁴A₂→⁴T₂ (4 070), ν₂ = ⁴A₂→⁴T₁(F) (7 030), and ν₃ = ⁴A₂→⁴T₁(P) (16 400 cm⁻¹).\n- Tetrahedral d⁷ has the same term structure as octahedral d³ (with the sign of Dq reversed), so I fit the bands with the d³ octahedral model.\n- The spin-allowed fit reproduces all three bands with residuals under 2 cm⁻¹ (R² ≈ 1.0). The ratio-based fit gives B ≈ 772 cm⁻¹, consistent with the full fit.\n- The fitted parameter is Dq_eff = 407 cm⁻¹, so the tetrahedral splitting is Δt = 10Dq ≈ 4 070 cm⁻¹. Taken as a magnitude, Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹ under the convention 10Dq = Δt.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The tool reports C = 4 133 cm⁻¹, but these three bands do not constrain C, so I am not giving a C value.\n- B ≈ 750 cm⁻¹ is about 67% of the free-ion Co²⁺ value (~1 120 cm⁻¹), which indicates moderate covalency (nephelauxetic reduction).\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of the three bands as tetrahedral d7 (mapped to octahedral d3) gives Dq≈407 and B≈748 cm-1; C is not constrained by these bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7578,"cache_read_input_tokens":7974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7578,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30ecfd9f-2333-4838-b7a2-ada7